{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/928019-anonymity-and-alternate-identity","id":928019,"title":"Anonymity and Alternate Identity","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-07-08T01:53:21-07:00","id":928019,"title":"Anonymity and Alternate Identity","content":"I've been thinking about a couple of Merits in the book that seem to me a bit problematic - or at least potentially redundant.<br /> This is largely dependant on the Storyteller's interpretation and group you're playing with, of course, but there is something that bothers me a bit about the Merits themselves and I wanted to share it with you guys to see what you thought. <br /> <br /> <b>Alternate Identity:</b> <br /> I can understand the necessity of a mechanical trait representing a false identity that is supported by forged documents and database entries. That's fine. What I don't get is the first dot. <br /> All the first dot represents is (and I quote) &quot;your character uses an alias with a change of clothes and adopts an accent&quot; - do you really need a Merit dot for that? <i>Anyone</i> can give a false name in an introduction and pretend they're someone they're not. Accents and clothes aren't so exotic that they necessitate a special Merit, to me. Especially if we're talking about just giving a false name or adopting an accent and attire that isn't so far off from the character's - like a New Yorker adopting a Jersey accent or a stereotypical suit-and-tie Upper Class guy keeping a pair of jeans and a t-shirt. <br /> The first dot level just feels like something that can be achieved basically at a drop of a hat (Bob from Orlando: <i>&quot;My name is John Smith from Delaware&quot;</i>). The first dot of the Merit would make more sense to me if it represented something more drastic (a girl who never left Small Town in Midwest America adopting a perfect Ukrainian accent and a convincing story). Even then, it would still feel a bit unnecessary to have to spend Merit dots on something like that.<br /> <br /> <b>Anonymity:</b><br /> Ok, so let's say my character decides to go live life as a wolf in the wild (one of the examples suggested by the Merit description) - now, wait a minute... the Merit is supposed to inflict itself in its own rating as a penalty specifically to attempts to track down my character using a paper trail. Since the Merit doesn't come with a scale (one dot, you mostly use cash - five dots, you shed off all human identity and run around the northern woods in Urhan), all it does is give a -1 to -5 to an Investigation roll. <br /> But if I've run off to the wilderness in wolf form <b><i>how is it even possible to track my movements with a paper trail</i></b>? If I've absolutely went Hunter in Darkness wilderness wolf, shouldn't a roll to track me using a paper trail simply be <i>impossible </i>to make or an automatic failure?<br /> <br /> The Merit seems to work for cases where a character still lives in human society (like most werewolves) but avoids things like credit cards and Facebook, the example of a werewolf running to the wilds to live as a wolf seems to merit something much, much more drastic than this Merit can account for. <br /> <br /> It also feels like the Merit itself is a bit arbitrary - <i>&quot;I don't use a credit card, so here's a flat -1 you take on rolls to find me using my credit card&quot;</i>, well, in that case won't the roll just be an auto-fail? I mean, I'm trying to track you using your credit history when you clearly haven't had one in the past six months. You could have run off to the other side of the country, I wouldn't know that just looking up stuff from your credit card. <br /> The opposite also works - you don't use a credit card, but you're a hilariously reckless driver and you can't go a mile without accruing enough traffic violations and parking tickets to cobble a road from. Won't the Merit be inapplicable in the case of such a character?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1807-serpent-axis","name":"Serpent Axis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1807_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"135"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T09:29:51-07:00","id":928207,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Serpent Axis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/928019-anonymity-and-alternate-identity#post928019\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What I don't get is the first dot.</div> </div> </div> </div>The big thing you seem to be skipping with this in your analysis is that it includes time spent creating the identity. Anyone can walk up and lie about who they are. Someone with Alternate Identity even at one dot has people around that know them by that name. The character has spent time on the persona and establishing its existence. So when someone comes to your part of the world looking for you with your real name and a vague description, nobody knows who that person is talking about, because they all know you as Bob not this other person. That's not something you can do on the spot.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i>Anyone</i> can give a false name in an introduction and pretend they're someone they're not. </div> </div> </div>I think this needs to be put in context. Mundane one dot/first dot Merits are rarely &quot;new&quot; abilities compared to minor boosts to existing Skills. So the first dot not doing that much (esp. nothing mechanical) isn't really something that stands out.<br /> <br /> As well, your analysis (for both Merits) seems to be drastically skipping the idea that Merits <i>add</i> to your character. In the vast majority of cases (esp. mundane Merits) they're bonuses to things anyone can do rather than new abilities. And an important part of that factor is that this means there's also a certain amount of stacking that goes on. You don't need a Merit to do X, having the Merit just makes doing X easier. You don't need Alternate Identity to disguise yourself as a different person, it just makes it easier because even at the first level that persona &quot;exists&quot; in the minds of other people. You seem to be treating Merits as required for doing things; which is only the case if they say they are. Most of the time they're meant as boosts to doing things. Anyone can theoretically use their Skills to get a dot rated Cash Equipment, to buy an Availability 5 item. Someone with Resources 5 can just buy it when they want and spend their time doing other things.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> But if I've run off to the wilderness in wolf form <b><i>how is it even possible to track my movements with a paper trail</i></b>? If I've absolutely went Hunter in Darkness wilderness wolf, shouldn't a roll to track me using a paper trail simply be <i>impossible </i>to make or an automatic failure? </div> </div> </div>Possibly, but again, you're skipping that Merits are additive. If your character lives off the grid, the ST should levee a penalty against any attempts to track you down as it is. Anonymity just adds even more penalties. You character still has a paper trail to chase down (they weren't born a wolf after all), but between the Merit and any situational penalties the ST feels the investigator should have, the vast majority of investigators are going to be in chance-die territory anyway.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It also feels like the Merit itself is a bit arbitrary - <i>&quot;I don't use a credit card, so here's a flat -1 you take on rolls to find me using my credit card&quot;</i>, well, in that case won't the roll just be an auto-fail? I mean, I'm trying to track you using your credit history when you clearly haven't had one in the past six months. You could have run off to the other side of the country, I wouldn't know that just looking up stuff from your credit card.<br /> The opposite also works - you don't use a credit card, but you're a hilariously reckless driver and you can't go a mile without accruing enough traffic violations and parking tickets to cobble a road from. Won't the Merit be inapplicable in the case of such a character? </div> </div> </div>The Merit doesn't force you to specify the kind of paper trail. The system generally doesn't abstract things so narrowly. You're not going to roll a credit history, then roll a background check, then roll a DMV records check, etc. etc. You're just going to make one Investigation roll (probably extended), and the Merit applies to paper trails in general.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-07-08T22:46:10-07:00","id":928602,"title":"","content":"The first dot of Alternate Identity is Keyzer Soze. It's also Matches Malone for Batman. It is more than just a hat and accent. Same with anonymity, yes, you can run off and not have a paper trail any longer. However, your paper trail going cold is *part of the paper trail*.<br /> <br /> Merits, or the benefits they provide, can be gained in game without buying the merits. The dots offer more than a skill roll can pull off, usually, or they're more reliable than always having to make a roll.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://imagizer.imageshack.us/v2/137x125q90/745/a5GS5M.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/802-amaterasu","name":"amaterasu","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar802_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"208"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}